## Validation Status

Every published figure from the source paper's Section 3 experiment is reproduced to within its publication precision.

| Target | Paper | This implementation | Deviation |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Table 3** — min-variance portfolio | `[0.452013, 0.115573, 0.432414]` | `[0.452011, 0.115573, 0.432415]` | `1.7e-06` |
| **Table 3** — portfolio variance | `0.00378529` | `0.00378529` | `1.2e-09` |
| **Table 4** — VaR at `β` = 0.95 | `0.090200` | `0.090199` | `9.3e-07` |
| **Table 4** — CVaR at `β` = 0.95 | `0.115908` | `0.115908` | `2.9e-07` |
| **Table 6** — Sobol LP, `q >= 10,000` | "< 1% deviation" | max `0.26%` | claim holds |
| **Table 5** — pseudo-random MC | "slow at best" | sd 1.0–1.3% at `q` = 20,000 (20 seeds) | claim holds |

The validation design exploits the paper's own **Proposition**: under normality with `β >= 0.5` and an active return constraint, the minimum-CVaR, minimum-VaR and minimum-variance portfolios coincide. The Markowitz QP therefore provides an *exact analytic benchmark*, independent of the LP being tested.

Theorems 1 and 2 are additionally verified numerically — the LP optimal value equals a brute-force `min_α F~_β` and an independent order-statistic estimate to **better than 1e-12**, with `α*` coinciding with the left endpoint of the argmin at every `β`.

