## Provenance & Attribution

This repository is **not** a pure reproduction of a single paper. Rockafellar & Uryasev (2000) supplies the baseline risk engine; the multi-objective and stress-testing layers draw on separate literatures, and some components are original to this project. The boundaries are set out below so that nothing here is mistaken for a result it is not.

### Derived from Rockafellar & Uryasev (2000)

The reference paper, reproduced and validated:

| Component | Source |
|---|---|
| Loss function `f(x,y) = -x'y` | eq. (12) |
| Auxiliary function `F_β(x,α)` | eq. (4), eq. (13) |
| Scenario approximation `F~_β` | eq. (9), eq. (17) |
| LP reduction: `u_k >= 0`, `x'y_k + α + u_k >= 0` | text following eq. (17) |
| Budget and no-short-selling constraints | eq. (11) |
| Expected-return floor | eq. (15) |
| Parametric VaR/CVaR coefficients `c₁(β)`, `c₂(β)` | eqs. (18)–(19) |
| CVaR = `min_α F_β`; VaR = left endpoint of the argmin | Theorem 1 |
| Equivalence of the joint and CVaR minimisations | Theorem 2 |
| Equivalence of (P1), (P2), (P3) under normality | Proposition, Section 3 |
| Mean returns, covariance matrix | Tables 1–2, verbatim |
| Minimum-variance benchmark and its VaR/CVaR | Tables 3–4, verbatim |
| Pseudo-random vs quasi-random convergence claims | Tables 5–6 |
| NIKKEI book: instruments, strikes, maturities, positions, marks | Table 7, verbatim |
| Hedging loss form `f(x,y) = x'(m - y)` | eq. (23) |
| "The normal distribution fits the data poorly" | Figure 1, qualitative claim only |

### Derived from other published work

These are **not** in the reference paper. Each is cited in full under [Academic References](#academic-references):

| Component | Source |
|---|---|
| Exact discrete-atom CVaR, `CVaR = λ·VaR + (1-λ)·CVaR⁺` | **Rockafellar & Uryasev (2002)** — a *different* paper. The 2000 paper assumes no probability atoms, which fails for a finite scenario set, so the later formulation is used |
| Coherence axioms motivating CVaR over VaR | Artzner, Delbaen, Eber & Heath (1999) |
| Minimum-variance benchmark problem (P3) | Markowitz (1952) |
| NSGA-II: fast non-dominated sorting, crowding distance, constrained domination | Deb, Pratap, Agarwal & Meyarivan (2002) |
| Simulated binary crossover (SBX), polynomial mutation | Deb & Agrawal (1995) |
| Simplex-lattice weight design | Das & Dennis (1998) |
| Augmented Tchebycheff scalarisation | Miettinen (1999) |
| Hypervolume and coverage indicators | Zitzler & Thiele (1999) |
| Option pricing and Greeks | Black & Scholes (1973); Merton (1973) |
| Source of the NIKKEI hedging problem | Mausser & Rosen (1999) |

### Original to this project

No published source. These are design decisions and modelling choices made here:

- **The entire multi-objective layer's problem formulation.** The reference paper contains no multi-objective optimisation. The return / CVaR / hedging-cost triple, the `HedgingCostModel` (proportional spread on turnover plus carry on gross exposure), and the `LinearEpigraph` protocol that makes the Tchebycheff programme exactly solvable are all original.
- **The entire stress-testing layer.** The paper contains no stress testing. The risk-factor taxonomy, the five historical scenarios and every shock magnitude in them, all factor betas (`SPX` 1.00, `GOVT` −10.0, `SMALL` 1.35, and the rest), and the bond duration/convexity figures are constructed, not estimated from data or taken from a source.
- **The three-way revaluation comparison** (delta / delta-gamma / full) and the Taylor convergence-order tests are standard practice, not a result from this paper.
- **Four assumptions underpinning the NIKKEI reconstruction**, none of them published: the contract multiplier of 1,000 (*inferred* from Table 7's internal consistency), the JPY risk-free rate of 0.005, the Mitsubishi/Komatsu correlation of 0.50, and the Student-t(4) return driver. The implied-volatility inversion is also a reconstruction technique, not the paper's method.
- **Packaging, CLI, CI and figure generation.**

### Summary

> The baseline risk engine is a faithful implementation of Rockafellar & Uryasev (2000), validated against its published tables. The multi-objective and stress-testing layers are original extensions built on separate literatures, and the NIKKEI loss distribution is a reconstruction, not a reproduction.

That is the strongest claim this repository supports. Anything broader — "all components follow the reference paper", "reproduces Figure 1", "reproduces the Section 4 results" — is not accurate.

