## Installation

Requires **Python 3.12+**.

```bash
# 1. Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/Akshit-Aggarwal05/Optimization-of-CVaR.git
cd Optimization-of-CVaR

# 2. Create and activate a virtual environment
python -m venv .venv

#    Windows (PowerShell)
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
#    macOS / Linux
source .venv/bin/activate

# 3. Install the package (editable, with test extras)
pip install --upgrade pip
pip install -e ".[dev]"

# 4. Verify the installation
pytest tests/
cvar-engine validate
```

Expected output:

```
225 passed in 160.82s

All 10 checks passed.
```

Installing the package puts the `cvar-engine` console script on your path and
makes `src` importable from anywhere. `pip install -r requirements.txt` still
works if you prefer not to install the package, but then commands must be run
from the repository root.

### Dependencies

| Package | Minimum | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `numpy` | 1.26 | Vectorized numerics throughout |
| `scipy` | 1.11 | `optimize.linprog` (HiGHS) for every LP; `sparse` for the scenario block; `stats.qmc` for Sobol sequences; `stats.norm` for Black–Scholes |
| `pandas` | 2.0 | Optional — imported lazily by the `.to_frame()` report helpers only |
| `matplotlib` | 3.8 | Figure generation (see [Results](#results--visualization)) |
| `pytest` | 7.4 | Test suite |

> **No commercial solver is required.** The linear programs are solved by HiGHS, which ships with SciPy. The source paper used CPLEX; results agree to solver tolerance.

