## Results & Visualization

All figures are generated by [`src/visualization.py`](src/visualization.py). Regenerate the whole set with `python -m src.visualization`, or one at a time with `cvar-engine figures --only <name>`.

### Distribution of Losses, NIKKEI Portfolio — reconstruction of Figure 1

![NIKKEI Loss Distribution](docs/images/nikkei_loss_distribution.png)

The paper's Figure 1 motivates its Section 4: this book's loss distribution is one "the normal distribution fits poorly", which is why Minimum CVaR and Minimum Variance can disagree for it. The reconstruction reproduces that finding statistically — Jarque-Bera rejects normality at **p = 1.2e-19**, with visible excess kurtosis against the fitted Gaussian.

**This is a reconstruction, not a reproduction.** The eleven instruments, strikes, maturities, positions and marks are Table 7 verbatim, and the implied volatilities are inverted from those marks so the base book reprices to the published total to 5e-7. The 1,000 scenarios behind the original figure belonged to Algorithmics and are not published, so they are simulated here. Magnitudes therefore differ from the paper's — see [Scope & Limitations](#scope--limitations).

```bash
cvar-engine nikkei --scenarios 1000 --plot
```

### Mean-CVaR Efficient Frontier

Because the problem is convex, every point on this frontier is a **global** optimum.

![Mean-CVaR Efficient Frontier](docs/images/efficient_frontier.png)

Regenerate with:

```python
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import numpy as np
from src.data.paper_datasets import MEAN_RETURNS, sample_normal_returns
from src.models.risk_metrics import PortfolioConstraints, cvar_efficient_frontier

returns = sample_normal_returns(8_192, engine="sobol", seed=20_000)
frontier = cvar_efficient_frontier(
    returns, beta=0.95, target_returns=np.linspace(0.0055, 0.0135, 25),
    constraints=PortfolioConstraints(budget=1.0, lower_bounds=0.0),
    expected_returns=MEAN_RETURNS,
)
plt.plot([p.cvar for p in frontier], [p.expected_return for p in frontier], "o-")
plt.xlabel("CVaR 0.95")
plt.ylabel("Expected return")
plt.title("Mean-CVaR Efficient Frontier")
plt.savefig("docs/images/efficient_frontier.png", dpi=150, bbox_inches="tight")
```

### Three-Objective Pareto Front

![Pareto Front](docs/images/pareto_front.png)

The exact Tchebycheff-LP front (red) against NSGA-II (blue). Every red point is a *global* optimum, so the genetic algorithm's points can lie on the surface or behind it, never in front of it.

### Portfolio Weight Distribution Along the Frontier

![Weight Distribution](docs/images/weight_distribution.png)

How capital rotates out of government bonds and into small caps as the return target rises — the allocation story behind the frontier curve.

### NSGA-II Convergence

![Convergence](docs/images/nsga2_convergence.png)

Normalised hypervolume by generation. The two-objective run climbs and settles; the three-objective run plateaus early and then wanders — the crowding-distance limitation that motivates keeping an exact reference front.

### Stress Test P&L Attribution

![Stress Attribution](docs/images/stress_attribution.png)

P&L by revaluation method across every historical scenario. **The gap between the bars is the linearisation error** — a delta-only risk report on a book with options is wrong by the visible amount, and the error grows with the square of the shock.

